An example test checks one input you picked. A property test states a rule that must hold for every input. It generates many inputs and shrinks a failing input to a small counterexample.

Effect 4 ships its own engine in `effect/Arbitrary`. `Arbitrary.schema` derives a generator from an Effect `Schema`. The same schema defines the [capability's contract](/systems/capabilities) and generates its test inputs. `Arbitrary.checkEffect` checks a pure or effectful property and "shrinks the first falsification." [Arbitrary.ts](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/67ba4e46a11ccda0b6761578bfd22c04ae00167d/packages/effect/src/Arbitrary.ts)

`@effect/vitest` exposes it as `it.prop` and `it.effect.prop`.

The [capture-first](/lore/capture-first) events cartridge is tested this way. For any generated request:

- Exactly one event is recorded.
- The response body and status are preserved.
- Persistent HTML may add the visitor cookie.
- The request body never reaches the event.
 For any query string, credential and email keys never survive. A failing sink does not change the response body or status. [Analytics](/systems/analytics) documents cookie behavior and what the checks establish about storage in Basin.

A property earns its place only if breaking the code breaks it. When first written, the events tests did not fail when `token` was removed from the sensitive-key list. The property compared the filter with the same function it was testing.

A second property with an independent list of sensitive keys closed the gap. The failure shrank to a single input, `"secret": "token"`. See [tests that earn their place](/lore/tests-that-earn-their-place).

For sequences of actions, use [model-based testing](/lore/model-based-testing).

See also: [Tests through supplied services](/lore/tests-that-earn-their-place).
